1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,420
The ad looks professional.

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,200
The person on screen sounds familiar.

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:06,060
The comments say it works.

4
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:11,540
Alex thinks: if this is a scam, why is a
major platform showing it to me?

5
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:13,800
That question just became a lawsuit.

6
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:19,920
On October twenty-second, twenty
twenty-six, Rhode Island sued Meta over

7
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:20,800
scam advertising.

8
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:22,560
According to Reuters,

9
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:27,940
the state alleges Meta profited from
fraudulent ads and helped create or

10
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:29,140
optimize some of them.

11
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:32,460
Meta disputes the case and says it will
fight it.

12
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:38,080
The state is seeking penalties, money back
for consumers, and changes to the

13
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:38,860
company's practices.

14
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:44,220
Those are requests in a civil lawsuit, not
a judgment already entered.

15
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:49,100
As of this episode, October twenty-third,
these allegations remain

16
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:49,600
contested.

17
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:55,440
Meta calls the lawsuit unfounded and says
it ignores the company's work

18
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:56,140
against scams.

19
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:01,053
In its own March announcement, Meta said
it removed more than one hundred

20
00:01:01,053 --> 00:01:05,760
fifty-nine million scam ads in twenty
twenty-five and was expanding

21
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:07,300
advertiser verification.

22
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:10,160
That removal count matters.

23
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:14,940
But it doesn't tell you what fraction of
scam ads were caught, or whether

24
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:16,900
the ad in front of Alex is safe.

25
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:19,760
The state challenges the system's conduct.

26
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:22,000
Meta points to its defenses.

27
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:26,180
Evidence and legal arguments will have to
resolve the dispute.

28
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:31,100
One example described in Reuters'
reporting involves ads

29
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:35,780
impersonating a bank executive and leading
users toward bogus stock

30
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:36,800
trades on WhatsApp.

31
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:42,180
The state's allegation is that familiar
names and institutions were used to

32
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:42,840
borrow trust.

33
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:46,620
It is not that the real executive endorsed
the scheme.

34
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:49,480
Now pause on Alex's question.

35
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:52,800
A paid placement tells you you're looking
at an advertisement.

36
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:57,120
It doesn't prove the seller is honest, the
person is really

37
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:00,160
involved, or the promised result will
happen.

38
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:04,600
A familiar platform and a trustworthy
offer are two separate things.

39
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:07,560
Let's follow a fictional example.

40
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:11,160
This is not an actual victim or an ad from
the lawsuit.

41
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:15,080
Alex sees an investment club promising
easy profits.

42
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:19,260
The video features an invented expert, an
impressive backdrop,

43
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:21,480
and a button marked Learn More.

44
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:23,720
The video looks convincing.

45
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:29,120
But financial regulators warn that
scammers can impersonate public figures

46
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:32,400
and use AI-generated images or video.

47
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:36,660
Looking for a strange blink or a broken
hand isn't enough.

48
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,620
You need a way to check the claim outside
the clip.

49
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:42,780
Below it, little messages seem to agree.

50
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:44,000
I got paid.

51
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,040
This changed my life.

52
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:49,240
In our example, those accounts are part of
the pitch.

53
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:53,600
A wall of praise can feel like evidence
even when you haven't verified

54
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:55,640
a single person behind it.

55
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:59,880
Alex taps the button and is invited into a
messaging group.

56
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:05,080
Suddenly there is a helpful administrator
and a stream of successful trades.

57
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:10,420
California's attorney general has warned
about social media investment ads

58
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:14,660
that move people into messaging apps and
fraudulent investment schemes.

59
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:19,940
In the fictional group, the administrator
offers to guide Alex through a

60
00:03:19,940 --> 00:03:20,600
small deposit.

61
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:22,720
The amount feels manageable.

62
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:26,440
A dashboard appears, and the balance
starts rising.

63
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:31,080
But a number drawn on a screen isn't
evidence that an investment exists

64
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:33,140
or that money can be withdrawn.

65
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:36,000
Then Alex asks to withdraw.

66
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:37,620
A new message arrives:

67
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:39,820
first, pay a release fee.

68
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:43,200
Maybe it's called a tax or verification
charge.

69
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:48,440
Regulators warn that investment scams can
invent extra payments when people

70
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:49,700
try to get their money back.

71
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:51,960
Sending more can deepen the loss.

72
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:54,100
Pull the camera back.

73
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:59,480
The polished ad, borrowed identity,
apparent crowd, private conversation,

74
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:02,480
and fake balance all supported one
journey.

75
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:07,300
That is why looking only at the final
payment can miss how trust was built

76
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:08,340
before the money moved.

77
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:13,550
The FTC says people reported losing two
point one billion dollars

78
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:17,720
to scams that started on social media in
twenty

79
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:21,120
twenty-five. That includes different
platforms and different kinds of

80
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:21,520
contact.

81
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:27,180
It is not a Meta-only figure, not an
ad-only figure, and not a court

82
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:28,460
finding against Meta.

83
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:34,100
Don't combine that dollar figure with
Meta's ad-removal count to calculate a

84
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:38,420
safety score. One counts reported losses
across social media.

85
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:41,720
The other counts ads the company says it
removed.

86
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,220
Different units, different coverage,
different questions.

87
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,840
Neither tells Alex to trust this
particular pitch.

88
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:51,620
The useful questions are concrete.

89
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:54,860
Can a platform identify the advertiser?

90
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:57,680
Can it stop a removed scammer from
returning?

91
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:59,860
What happens after a report?

92
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:04,380
Meta says it is expanding verification and
anti-scam technology.

93
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:09,060
The lawsuit challenges whether its conduct
and safeguards have been

94
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:09,460
adequate.

95
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:14,000
For Alex, the next move is outside the
ad's little world.

96
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:17,660
He closes the link, looks up the
institution independently,

97
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:21,540
and uses contact information from its
official website.

98
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:25,100
He checks whether the person actually made
the recommendation.

99
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:28,300
The ad doesn't get to supply its own
proof.

100
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:30,400
Guaranteed returns,

101
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:35,720
a secret opportunity, or pressure to
transfer money now are reasons to

102
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:36,340
slow down.

103
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:41,000
A real logo and an active chat group don't
cancel those warnings.

104
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:46,040
If the opportunity can't survive an
independent check, it doesn't deserve

105
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:46,640
your deposit.

106
00:05:47,470 --> 00:05:49,760
If you've already paid, act promptly.

107
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:53,740
Contact your bank or the payment service
and explain what happened.

108
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:58,720
Keep the ad, account names, messages,
website address, and transaction

109
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:02,980
records. Report the account to the
platform and the scam to the FTC.

110
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:05,540
Recovery is not guaranteed.

111
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:11,040
And watch for the second pitch: someone
claiming they can recover everything

112
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:12,160
for an upfront fee.

113
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:16,060
The FTC warns about refund and recovery
scams.

114
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:20,460
Losing money doesn't mean you should hand
the next stranger your wallet too.

115
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:24,640
Back at the beginning, Alex still sees the
polished ad.

116
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:29,740
This time he doesn't try to judge honesty
from the lighting, the voice, or

117
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:33,500
the comment count. He asks a smaller,
stronger question:

118
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:37,360
what can I verify somewhere this
advertiser doesn't control?

119
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:42,700
The Rhode Island case will turn on
evidence and the law, not how

120
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:44,540
persuasive a headline sounds.

121
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:49,240
We should watch the court process and
Meta's responses without turning

122
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:50,780
allegations into a verdict.

123
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:55,020
The practical lesson can stand while the
legal dispute continues.

124
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:57,960
A scam doesn't need to look suspicious.

125
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:01,440
It needs to look familiar long enough for
the next click.

126
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:04,000
A paid ad can buy your attention.

127
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:08,700
Before it earns your money, make it earn
your trust somewhere outside the

128
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:08,940
ad.
